 Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le4\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nm\\neq0,\\qquad m\\neq\\infty\\ (\\text{vertical}),\\qquad m\\neq-1 .\n\\]\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exist three distinct lines\n\\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly two of the lines are sunny.\n\nConsequently one of the three lines is **non\u2011sunny**, i.e. it is parallel to\nthe \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  We consider the three\npossibilities.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1  \n*Any line whose slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\) contains at most two\npoints of \\(T_{3}\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nTake any two distinct points of \\(T_{3}\\).  Their coordinates differ by at most\n\\(2\\) in each component, because all coordinates are in \\(\\{1,2,3\\}\\).  Hence\nthe slope between any two points belongs to the finite set  \n\n\\[\n\\{-2,-1,0,1,2,\\infty\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf a line is sunny, its slope cannot be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore the\nonly admissible slopes are \\(-2,1,2\\).  A direct inspection of the six points\nshows that no three of them are collinear with any of these three slopes.\nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The non\u2011sunny line is horizontal (\\(y=c\\))\n\nThe only values of \\(c\\) intersecting \\(T_{3}\\) are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  \n  \\(y=1\\) covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n  The uncovered set is  \n\n  \\[\n  R=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Slopes between pairs of points of \\(R\\) are respectively\n  \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), \\(0\\) (horizontal) and \\(-1\\); all are forbidden for\n  sunny lines.  Hence a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(R\\).\n  Two sunny lines can therefore cover at most two points of \\(R\\), leaving at\n  least one point uncovered \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(y=2\\) covers \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n  The uncovered set is  \n\n  \\[\n  R=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Compute the slopes of all pairs in \\(R\\):\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{pair} & \\text{slope} \\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1)-(1,3) & \\infty \\\\\n  (1,1)-(2,1) & 0 \\\\\n  (1,1)-(3,1) & 0 \\\\\n  (1,3)-(2,1) & -2 \\ (\\text{allowed})\\\\\n  (1,3)-(3,1) & -1 \\\\\n  (2,1)-(3,1) & 0\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  The only admissible pair for a sunny line is \\((1,3),(2,1)\\); the line\n  through them is \\(y=-2x+5\\).  After using this line, the two points\n  \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) remain uncovered.  Any line containing both would be\n  horizontal (\\(m=0\\)), i.e. non\u2011sunny.  Thus a second sunny line cannot cover\n  both, and we would need a third sunny line \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  \\(y=3\\) covers only \\((1,3)\\).  The five remaining points must be covered\n  by the two sunny lines.  By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can contain at most two\n  points, so together they can cover at most four points, leaving at least one\n  uncovered \u2013 contradiction.\n\nHence a horizontal non\u2011sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The non\u2011sunny line is vertical (\\(x=c\\))\n\nThe situation is symmetric to the horizontal case (swap the coordinates).\nExactly the same contradictions arise for \\(c=1,2,3\\).  Therefore a vertical\nnon\u2011sunny line is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The non\u2011sunny line has slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\))\n\nThe only values of \\(c\\) intersecting \\(T_{3}\\) are \\(c=2,3,4\\).\n\n* **\\(c=4\\).**  \n  The line \\(x+y=4\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) covers the three points\n  \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  The uncovered set is  \n\n  \\[\n  R=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  As in the horizontal case \\(c=1\\), every pair of points of \\(R\\) has a\n  forbidden slope (\\(\\infty,0,-1\\)).  Consequently each sunny line can contain\n  at most one point of \\(R\\); two sunny lines can cover at most two points,\n  leaving one uncovered \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  The line \\(x+y=3\\) covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  The remaining points are  \n\n  \\[\n  R=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Among all pairs of points in \\(R\\) the only admissible slope is \\(1\\),\n  occurring for the pair \\((1,1),(2,2)\\).  Thus one sunny line can be the line\n  \\(y=x\\) through these two points.  The two points left, \\((1,3)\\) and\n  \\((3,1)\\), have slope \\(-1\\) and therefore cannot lie on a sunny line\n  together.  Each would require its own sunny line, which would give three\n  sunny lines in total \u2013 contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  The line \\(x+y=2\\) covers only \\((1,1)\\).  The five remaining points must be\n  covered by the two sunny lines, but by Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can contain at\n  most two points, so at most four points can be covered \u2013 contradiction.\n\nThus a non\u2011sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) also leads to impossibility.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible type of the single non\u2011sunny line yields a contradiction,\nthe assumption that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists is\nfalse.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
