e line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y-s_j x = b_j-s_j a_j ,\n   \\qquad\\text{where }P_j=(a_j,b_j).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(S_j\\) passes through its designated point \\(P_j\\) and, because its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), it is a sunny line.  \n   The lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_m\\) are distinct (different slopes) and together cover all points of the removed columns.\n\n   *Step\u202f3.4 \u2013 Add dummy sunny lines if necessary.*  \n   If \\(m<k\\) we still need exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Choose any \\(k-m\\) additional sunny lines that do **not** pass through any point of \\(T_n\\) (for example, lines \\(y=2x+N\\) with a huge constant \\(N\\)). These extra lines are distinct from the previous ones because we give them slopes not used before.\n\n   *Step\u202f3.5 \u2013 Count the lines.*  \n   We have used  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-t) \\text{ vertical lines } + k \\text{ sunny lines } = n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, satisfying the required total of \\(n\\).\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Impossibility for \\(k>n\\).**  \n   The problem demands exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines. Since each line is either sunny or non\u2011sunny, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines; therefore \\(k>n\\) is impossible.\n\nCombining the existence proof for all \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) with the trivial impossibility for \\(k>n\\) yields the final answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition of the problem is that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_n\\;\\exists L\\in\\mathcal L\\text{ with }(a,b)\\in L .\n\\tag{A}\n\\