            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition of the problem is that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\forall (a,b)\\in T_n\\;\\exists L\\in\\mathcal L\\text{ with }(a,b)\\in L .\n\\tag{A}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); equivalently its slope is not \\(0\\), not \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and not \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nThus (A) holds with exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines. Consequently \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Preparations for the general case\n\nFor a fixed column index \\(a\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_a:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid b\\ge1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the column \\(C_a\\) contains precisely  \n\n\\[\n|C_a|=n+1-a\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints.  \nHence the right‑most \\(t\\) columns \\(C_{n-t+1},\\dots ,C_n\\) contain  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{t}(i)=\\frac{t(t+1)}2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nt:=\\max\\{\\,m\\in\\mathbb N\\mid \\tfrac{m(m+1)}2\\le k\\,\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\le n\\), we have \\(t\\le n\\).  \nFrom (2) and (3),\n\n\\[\nm:=\\frac{t(t+1)}2\\le k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nP_1,P_2,\\dots ,P_m\n\\]\n\nthe (distinct) points belonging to the right‑most \\(t\\) columns; thus the set \\(\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_m\\}\\) equals \\(\\bigcup_{a=n-t+1}^{n}C_a\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  Non‑sunny (vertical) lines\n\nFor each \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,n-t\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-t\\) lines are pairwise distinct and non‑sunny.  \nEvery point of the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-t}\\) lies on exactly one of them, so (A) is satisfied for all points outside the right‑most \\(t\\) columns.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  Sunny lines covering the removed columns\n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,m\\) write \\(P_j=(a_j,b_j)\\).  \nChoose pairwise different slopes  \n\n\\[\ns_j:=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,m),\n\\]\n\nwhich clearly avoid the forbidden values \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y-s_jx = b_j-s_j a_j .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEquation (5) guarantees that \\(P_j\\in S_j\\); the slope \\(s_j\\) makes \\(S_j\\) sunny.  \nBecause the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_m\\) are distinct.\n\nThus the \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(S_j\\) cover all points of the right‑most \\(t\\) columns.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  Adding dummy sunny lines\n\nIf \\(m<k\\) we still need exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nSelect any \\(k-m\\) additional slopes not used before and not equal to \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (for instance \\(s_{m+\\ell}=m+\\ell\\) for \\(\\ell=1,\\dots ,k-m\\)).  \nFor each such slope choose a line of the form  \n\n\\[\ny-s_{m+\\ell}x = C_\\ell ,\n\\]\n\nwhere the constant \\(C_\\ell\\) is taken so large that the line does not meet any point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThese extra lines are sunny and distinct from all previously constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.4  Counting the lines\n\nWe have built  \n\n* \\(n-t\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines,\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(m\\) lines \\(S_j\\) plus the \\(k-m\\) dummy lines).\n\nHence the total number of lines is \\((n-t)+k=n\\), as required.  \nCondition (A) holds because every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either in a column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le n-t\\) (covered by a vertical line) or in one of the right‑most \\(t\\) columns (covered by a sunny line).  \nExactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility for \\(k>n\\)\n\nThe problem stipulates that exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are used.  \nConsequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines; i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThus no \\(k>n\\) can satisfy the requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All }k=0,1,\\dots ,n\\text{ are possible, and no other }k\\text{ works.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
