ity for \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(C_j=\\{(n-j+1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le j\\}\\) be the \\((j\\)-th right‑most column of \\(T_n\\) (so \\(|C_j|=j\\)).  \n   If a sunny line contains a point of \\(C_{j+1}\\) then it cannot contain any point of \\(C_j\\).\n\n   *Proof of Lemma 1.*  \n   Take a point \\(P=(n-j,1)\\in C_{j+1}\\) and a point \\(Q=(n-j+1,1)\\in C_j\\).  \n   The line through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) is horizontal, slope \\(0\\), which is not sunny.  \n   Similarly the line through \\(P\\) and \\((n-j+1,2)\\in C_j\\) has slope \\(-1\\), also not sunny.  \n   Any other point of \\(C_j\\) has the same abscissa \\(n-j+1\\); a line joining it with a point of \\(C_{j+1}\\) must have slope either \\(0\\) (if the ordinate is \\(1\\)) or \\(-1\\) (if the ordinate is \\(2\\)). Hence a sunny line meeting \\(C_{j+1}\\) cannot meet \\(C_j\\). ∎\n\n   *Corollary.*  \n   To cover the points of the last \\(m\\) columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_m\\) we need at least  \n\n   \\[\n   1+2+\\dots +m=\\frac{m(m+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   distinct sunny lines, because each column \\(C_j\\) contains \\(j\\) points and, by Lemma 1, a sunny line that covers a point of a larger‑index column cannot be reused for a smaller‑index column.\n\n   In our problem we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. If \\(k\\ge2\\) we would have to cover the last \\(k\\) columns, i.e. \\(m=k\\). The corollary then forces  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\;,\n   \\]\n\n   which is false for every integer \\(k\\ge2\\) (the right‑hand side exceeds the left‑hand side). Hence no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines can satisfy (A).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines can cover all points of \\(T_n\\) while containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** 