ly the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  For which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) does there exist a\n> covering of the triangular set \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Even values of \\(k\\) are impossible (except \\(k=0\\))\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf \\(k\\ge 2\\) is even, no covering of \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the unique point of the right‑most column,\n\\(P_k=(k,1)\\).  Let \\(L\\) be the (supposed) sunny line containing \\(P_k\\).\nBecause the slope of \\(L\\) is different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), the second point of\n\\(L\\) in the left‑most column cannot be \\((1,1)\\) (horizontal line) nor\n\\((1,k)\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  Hence \\(L\\) meets the left‑most column at a point\n\\((1,t)\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k-1\\).\n\nDelete from \\(T_k\\) the whole right‑most column and the whole bottom row\n\\(\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k\\}\\).  The remaining set is precisely \\(T_{k-2}\\).\nThe line \\(L\\) has already covered the two points \\((k,1)\\) and \\((1,t)\\); all\nother points of \\(T_{k-2}\\) must be covered by the remaining \\(k-1\\) lines.\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is even, \\(k-1\\) is odd.  Repeating the same argument for the set\n\\(T_{k-2}\\) shows that any covering of \\(T_{k-2}\\) by \\(k-1\\) lines must contain a\nnon‑sunny line (otherwise the point \\((k-2,1)\\) would have to be on a sunny line\nthat also meets the left‑most column at \\((1,1)\\) or \\((1,k-2)\\), both\nforbidden).  Hence the original covering of \\(T_k\\) also contains a non‑sunny line,\ncontradicting the assumption that all \\(k\\) lines are sunny. ∎\n\nThe cases \\(k=0\\) (trivial) and \\(k=2\\) are covered separately:\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) contains no pair of points with a sunny slope,\nso two sunny lines cannot cover the three points.  Therefore **no even\n\\(k\\ge 2\\) is admissible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for every odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  Define, for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = m_i x + b_i,\\qquad\nm_i=\\frac{k+1-2i}{\\,k-1\\,},\\qquad\nb_i=\\frac{2i-1}{\\,k-1\\,}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k-1\\) is even, the numerator \\(k+1-2i\\) is also even; therefore\n\\(m_i\\) is an integer.  Moreover \\(m_i\\neq0\\) (otherwise \\(i=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) would give\n\\(k+1-2i=0\\), impossible for odd \\(k\\)), and \\(m_i\\neq-1\\) because\n\\(k+1-2i = -(k-1)\\) would imply \\(i=k\\), which gives \\(m_k= -1\\) only when\n\\(k=1\\); the case \\(k=1\\) is handled separately (any sunny line through\n\\((1,1)\\) works).  Hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nA direct substitution shows that \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nA_i=(i,i),\\qquad B_i=(k-i+1,1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\nm_i\\cdot i+b_i\n   =\\frac{k+1-2i}{k-1}\\,i+\\frac{2i-1}{k-1}\n   =i,\\qquad\nm_i\\cdot (k-i+1)+b_i\n   =\\frac{k+1-2i}{k-1}\\,(k-i+1)+\\frac{2i-1}{k-1}=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(k\\) lines are distinct (they have distinct slopes) and together\ncontain all points of the two “border” sets\n\\(\\{(i,i)\\mid1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) and \\(\\{(j,1)\\mid1\\le j\\le k\\}\\).\n\nNow take any point \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\).  Write \\(x-y=r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le k-1\\).\nSet \\(i=\\frac{k+1-r}{2}\\); because \\(k\\) is odd, \\(i\\) is an integer and\n\\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  Using (3) one checks that \\((x,y)\\) satisfies the equation of\n\\(L_i\\); consequently \\((x,y)\\in L_i\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on one\nof the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\nTherefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) cover the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Lifting the construction to \\(T_n\\)\n\nGiven \\(n\\ge k\\) with \\(k\\) odd, take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (all non‑sunny).  Translate each line \\(L_i\\) of the previous\nsection by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\); the translated lines are still sunny and now\ncover exactly the points of the right‑most \\(k\\) columns of \\(T_n\\).  Together the\n\\(n\\) lines satisfy condition (A) and contain precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nFor \\(k=0\\) we simply use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are\nnon‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Final answer\n\nCombining the impossibility for even \\(k\\ge2\\) with the explicit constructions\nfor \\(k=0\\) and for every odd \\(k\\le n\\), we obtain the complete description of\nthe admissible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{or}\\;k\\text{ is odd with }1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThus the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines exists is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb N\\mid\nk\\le n,\\;k\\text{ odd}\\}\\)."
