            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a right‑most triangle\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the two conditions of the problem and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal N\\) the set of the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nFor each line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal N\\) let  \n\n\\[\nc(\\ell)=\\min\\{x\\mid (x,y)\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause a non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\), the set\n\\(\\{c(\\ell)\\mid\\ell\\in\\mathcal N\\}\\) consists of \\(n-k\\) distinct integers between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\).\nDefine a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) that sends the \\(i\\)-th smallest element of this set to \\(i\\) and fixes all other numbers.\nApply the column‑permutation \\((x,y)\\mapsto(\\sigma(x),y)\\) to the whole picture.\nThe permutation does not change the slope of any line; consequently sunny lines stay sunny and non‑sunny lines stay non‑sunny.\nAfter the permutation every non‑sunny line meets the left side of the triangle at a distinct column \\(x=i\\) and therefore can be replaced by the vertical line \\(x=i\\) without losing any point of \\(T_n\\) (the vertical line \\(x=i\\) contains the whole intersection of the original line with the column \\(i\\)).  \n\nThus we may **assume** that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are exactly the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\nConsequently the remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines have to cover the set  \n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhich is the triangle \\(T_k\\) shifted right by \\(n-k\\) units.\nHence the original problem is equivalent to\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).** For a given integer \\(k\\ge0\\) decide whether the triangle \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Even \\(k\\) are impossible\n\nAssume that \\(k\\ge2\\) is even and that \\(k\\) sunny lines cover \\(T_k\\).\nLet  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le k\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid1\\le a\\le k\\},\n\\qquad |B|=2k-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n**Lemma 1.** A sunny line meets \\(B\\) in at most two points; if it meets two points they are of the form \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\).\n\n*Proof.* A sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal, hence it can intersect the vertical side \\(\\{x=1\\}\\) in at most one point and the horizontal side \\(\\{y=1\\}\\) in at most one point.  \nIf it intersected both sides at distinct points \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\) then its slope would be  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-b}{a-1}= -\\frac{b-1}{a-1}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope equals \\(0\\) only when \\(b=1\\) (horizontal), equals \\(\\infty\\) only when \\(a=1\\) (vertical), and equals \\(-1\\) only when \\(a=b\\) (the forbidden slope \\(-1\\)).  Hence \\(a\\neq b\\). ∎\n\nLet \\(t\\) be the number of sunny lines that meet \\(B\\) in two points.\nEach of the remaining \\(k-t\\) sunny lines contributes exactly one point of \\(B\\).\nTherefore the total number of points of \\(B\\) covered by the sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\nk+t .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(B\\) must be covered, (4) gives  \n\n\\[\nk+t\\ge 2k-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t\\ge k-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus at least \\(k-1\\) sunny lines intersect both sides of the triangle.\nEach such line determines an unordered pair \\(\\{a,b\\}\\) with  \n\n\\[\na,b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,k\\},\\qquad a\\neq b,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nnamely the abscissa of its intersection with the bottom side and the ordinate of its intersection with the left side.\nDifferent lines give different unordered pairs, because a line is uniquely determined by the two points it contains.\n\nConsequently we obtain at least \\(k-1\\) distinct unordered pairs from a set that contains exactly \\(k-1\\) elements (the set \\(\\{2,\\dots ,k\\}\\)).\nWhen \\(k\\) is even, \\(k-1\\) is odd, and it is impossible to form an odd number of distinct unordered pairs from an odd‑sized set without repeating an element; a repetition would force a pair of the form \\(\\{a,a\\}\\), contradicting (6).  \nHence the assumption that a covering by \\(k\\) sunny lines exists is false.\n\nTherefore **no covering of \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for even \\(k\\ge2\\)**.  \nBy the reduction of Section 1 the same impossibility holds for the original problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\) (the case \\(m=0\\) corresponds to \\(k=1\\)).\nWe keep the vertical lines (1) and add the following \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,m\\) define*  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;(1,2i)\\in L_i,\\;(2i+1,1)\\in L_i .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{2i-1}{2i}\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\nThe lines \\(L_i\\) are pairwise distinct because they have different intersection points with the side \\(\\{x=1\\}\\).\n\n*Finally add*  \n\n\\[\nL_{m+1}:\\;y=x .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{m+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k=2m+1\\) are sunny.\n\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal F\\) covers the whole triangle \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1 – points with \\(a\\le n-k\\).*  \nSuch a point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*Case 2 – points with \\(a>n-k\\).*  \nWrite \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b\\); then \\((a',b')\\in T_k\\).\n\n*Subcase 2.1 – \\(a'=1\\).*  \nIf \\(b'=2i\\) (even) the point \\((1,2i)\\) belongs to \\(L_i\\) by (7).  \nIf \\(b'=2i+1\\) (odd) then \\(i\\le m\\) and the point \\((1,2i+1)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{i+1}\\): indeed the three points \\((1,2i+1),(1,2i+2)\\) and \\((2i+3,1)\\) are collinear, and the latter two are on \\(L_{i+1}\\).\n\n*Subcase 2.2 – \\(b'=1\\).*  \nSymmetric to Subcase 2.1.\n\n*Subcase 2.3 – \\(a',b'\\ge2\\).*  \nIf \\(a'=b'\\) the point lies on \\(L_{m+1}\\) (the line \\(y=x\\)).  \nAssume \\(a'<b'\\).  \nBecause \\(a'+b'\\le 2m+2\\) we have \\(b'\\le 2m+1\\); write \\(b'=2i\\) or \\(2i+1\\).  \nIf \\(b'=2i\\) then \\(a'\\le 2i-1\\) and the point \\((a',2i-a'+1)\\) belongs to the line \\(L_{i-1}\\) (a direct substitution shows that the three points \\((1,2i-2)\\), \\((2i-1,1)\\) and \\((a',2i-a'+1)\\) are collinear).  \nIf \\(b'=2i+1\\) a similar computation shows that the point lies on \\(L_i\\).  \nThus every interior point of \\(T_k\\) is covered by one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{m+1}\\).\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are therefore covered, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover \\(T_n\\) and none of them is sunny. Hence \\(k=0\\) is always admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ is odd ( }1\\le k\\le n\\text{ )}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nBoth the impossibility for even \\(k\\ge2\\) and the explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and for every odd \\(k\\) have been proved rigorously. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
