e coordinates sum to an odd number \\(s\\) lies on the appropriate \\(L_i\\).\n\n   – If \\(s\\) is even, write \\(s=2i+2\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\).  \n     The line \\(M_i\\) passes through \\((1,2i+1)\\) and \\((2i+2,1)\\); its integer points are\n     \\[\n     (1+t,\\;2i+1-t),\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,2i+1 ,\n     \\]\n     all having sum \\(2i+2=s\\). Thus every point with even sum belongs to the appropriate \\(M_i\\).\n\n   – The unique point with \\(a=b=1\\) (sum \\(2\\)) lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\).\n\n   Consequently **every** point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n4. **The case \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and none of them is sunny. Hence \\(k=0\\) is always admissible.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   *All* admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(k=0\\) and the odd integers \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   The impossibility for even \\(k\\ge2\\) and the explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and odd \\(k\\) together give a complete solution. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
