es \u2013 a contradiction.  \n> Consequently **no covering exists for even \\(k\\ge 2\\).**\u201d\n\n**Analysis of the logical chain:**\n\n1. **Correct observation:** \\((1,k)\\) and \\((k,1)\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=k+1\\), which has slope \\(-1\\). Sunny lines are indeed not parallel to this line, so each sunny line intersects it in at most one point.\n\n2. **Faulty claim:** \u201cTo contain \\((1,k)\\) a line must also contain a point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\le1\\); the only possibility is the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\u201d  \n   - A sunny line that passes through \\((1,k)\\) need **not** pass through any point of the form \\((a,1)\\). It merely intersects the diagonal at \\((1,k)\\) and can intersect the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) at a different \\(x\\)-coordinate (or not intersect it at all if it is parallel to the horizontal, which is prohibited for sunny lines). The statement that the line must also contain a point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\le1\\) is unfounded.  \n   - Consequently, the conclusion that \\((1,k)\\) can only be covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is false. The same reasoning incorrectly forces \\((k,1)\\) to be covered only by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n3. **Resulting error:** The deduction \u201cany covering of \\(T_k\\) needs at least two non\u2011sunny lines\u201d is therefore **invalid**. Since the premise is false, the derived necessity of two non\u2011sunny lines does not hold.\n\n4. **Final contradiction:** The subsequent statement \u201cfor even \\(k\\) we would need \\(n-k\\ge2\\), i.e. at most \\(k-2\\) sunny lines \u2013 a contradiction\u201d rests entirely on the false premise identified in (2). Hence the impossibility proof for even \\(k\\) collapses.\n\n*Classification:* **Critical Error** \u2013 the argument contains a logical fallacy that breaks the chain of reasoning. The conclusion that even \\(k\\) is impossible is not justified.\n\n*Impact:* This error undermines the entire \u201cno even \\(k\\)\u201d part of the answer. Although the construction for odd \\(k\\) is correct, the overall claim that **only** odd \\(k\\) (and \\(k=0\\)) are admissible is not established.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Step\u202f3 \u2013 Construction for odd \\(k=2m+1\\)\n\n> \u201cKeep the vertical lines (1).  \n> Define the following \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover \\(T_k\\).  \n> *For \\(i=1,\\dots ,m\\)*  \n> \\[\n> L_i:\\;(1,2i)\\in L_i,\\;(2i+1,1)\\in L_i\n> \\qquad\\text{slope }-\\frac{2i-1}{2i}\\neq0,\\infty,-1 .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]  \n> *For \\(i=1,\\dots ,m\\)*  \n> \\[\n> M_i:\\;(1,2i+1)\\in M_i,\\;(2i+2,1)\\in M_i\n> \\qquad\\text{slope }-\\frac{2i}{2i+1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1 .\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]  \n> *Finally*  \n> \\[\n> D:\\;y=x\n> \\qquad\\text{slope }1\\neq0,\\infty,-1 .\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification of each sub\u2011step:**\n\n* **Slopes:**  \n  - For \\(L_i\\): slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-2i}{2i+1-1}=\\frac{1-2i}{2i}= -\\frac{2i-1}{2i}\\). This is never \\(0\\), never undefined, and never \\(-1\\) (solving \\(-\\frac{2i-1}{2i}=-1\\) gives \\(2i-1=2i\\), impossible). Hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n  - For \\(M_i\\): slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-(2i+1)}{2i+2-1}= \\frac{-2i}{2i+1}= -\\frac{2i}{2i+1}\\). Again not \\(0\\), not undefined, not \\(-1\\). Hence each \\(M_i\\) is sunny.  \n  - \\(D\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is allowed.  \n\n* **Distinctness:**  \n  - All vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) have infinite slope, distinct from each other and from the finite\u2011slope lines \\(L_i,M_i,D\\).  \n  - For different indices \\(i\\neq j\\), the slopes \\(-\\frac{2i-1}{2i}\\) and \\(-\\frac{2j-1}{2j}\\) are distinct, as are the slopes \\(-\\frac{2i}{2i+1}\\) and \\(-\\frac{2j}{2j+1}\\). None of the \\(L_i\\) coincide with any \\(M_j\\) because their slopes differ (the former are of the form \\(-\\frac{\\text{odd}}{\\text{even}}\\), the latter \\(-\\frac{\\text{even}}{\\text{odd}}\\)). \\(D\\) has slope \\(1\\), distinct from all others. Hence the family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n* **Count of sunny lines:**  \n  - Number of \\(L_i\\) = \\(m\\).  \n  - Number of \\(M_i\\) = \\(m\\).  \n  - Plus \\(D\\) = 1.  \n  - Total sunny = \\(2m+1 = k\\).  \n  - Number of vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines = \\(n-k\\).  \n  - Total lines = \\((n-k) + k = n\\).  \n\n* **Coverage of \\(T_k\\):**  \n  - **Points with \\(a=1\\):** For each \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le k\\):  \n    - If \\(b=2i\\) (even) then \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) by construction (3).  \n    - If \\(b=2i+1\\) (odd) then \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(M_i\\) by construction (4).  \n\n  - **Points with \\(b=1\\):** Symmetric argument: \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\) even belongs to some \\(M_i\\), \\(a\\) odd belongs to some \\(L_i\\).  \n\n  - **Points with \\(a,b\\ge2\\):** Let \\(s=a+b\\).  \n    - If \\(s\\) is odd, write \\(s=2i+1\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\). The integer points on \\(L_i\\) are \\((1+t,\\,2i-t)\\) for \\(t=0,\\dots,2i\\); each such point satisfies \\(a+b=2i+1=s\\). Hence every lattice point of \\(T_k\\) with odd sum \\(s\\) lies on the appropriate \\(L_i\\).  \n    - If \\(s\\) is even, write \\(s=2i+2\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\). The integer points on \\(M_i\\) are \\((1+t,\\,2i+1-t)\\) for \\(t=0,\\dots,2i+1\\); each satisfies \\(a+b=2i+2=s\\). Hence every lattice point of \\(T_k\\) with even sum \\(s\\) lies on the appropriate \\(M_i\\).  \n\n  - **Point \\((1,1)\\):** Has sum \\(2\\) (even) but is not covered by any \\(L_i\\) or \\(M_i\\) because those require \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\) with at least one coordinate equal to \\(1\\) and the other larger. The line \\(D\\) (the diagonal \\(y=x\\)) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and is sunny.  \n\n  Hence **every** point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*Verdict:* All steps of the construction for odd \\(k\\) are correct and rigorously justified.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Step\u202f4 \u2013 The case \\(k=0\\)\n\n> \u201cThe \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and none of them is sunny. Hence \\(k=0\\) is always admissible.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nFor any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\) (since \\(b\\ge1\\)). Thus the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (one of the lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\)) contains the point. All these lines are vertical, i.e. non\u2011sunny. The argument is complete.\n\n*Verdict:* Correct.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Step\u202f5 \u2013 Conclusion\n\n> \u201cAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(k=0\\) and the odd integers \\(1\\le k\\le n\\). The impossibility for even \\(k\\ge2\\) and the explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and odd \\(k\\) together give a complete solution.\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe conclusion relies on the impossibility proof for even \\(k\\). Since that proof contains a **Critical Error**, the statement that *no* even \\(k\\) works is **not established**. Therefore the overall answer is not justified.\n\n*Verdict:* Invalid due to reliance on the flawed argument.\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\n- The **construction for odd \\(k\\)** and the **case \\(k=0\\)** are correct and fully justified.\n- The **reduction step** (assuming all non\u2011sunny lines can be taken vertical) is not proved; this is a **Justification Gap**.\n- The **impossibility argument for even \\(k\\)** contains a **Critical Error**: it incorrectly asserts that the extreme points \\((1,k)\\) and \\((k,1)\\) force the use of non\u2011sunny lines. This logical mistake invalidates the claim that even \\(k\\) is impossible.\n- Because the final answer depends on the invalid impossibility proof, the solution as a whole is **incorrect**."

