k\\) lines to cover the diagonal \\(a+b=3\\) we have only \\(k-2\\) lines left.  \n\nThe diagonal \\(a+b=4\\) has three points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nEach of the remaining \\(k-2\\) lines can meet this diagonal in at most one point, so at most \\(k-2\\) of the three points can be covered.  \nBecause \\(k\\) is even, \\(k-2\\) is also even and therefore **strictly smaller** than \\(3\\).  Hence at least one point of the diagonal \\(a+b=4\\) remains uncovered – a contradiction.  \n\nTherefore no even positive \\(k\\) can cover \\(T_k\\).  \n\n*Construction for odd \\(k\\).*  \nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge0\\)).  Fix the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all required points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\).  \nFor the remaining triangle \\(T_k\\) define the sunny lines \\(L_i,M_i,D\\) as in (3)–(5).  \n\nAll slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the lines are sunny and distinct.  \n\n**Verification.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  \n\n*If \\(a=1\\):*  \n\\(b\\) is between \\(1\\) and \\(2m+1\\).  \nIf \\(b=2i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le m\\)) then \\((1,2i)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) by definition (3).  \nIf \\(b=2i+1\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le m\\)) then \\((1,2i+1)\\) lies on \\(M_i\\) by (4).  \n\n*If \\(b=1\\):* the argument is symmetric, using the second point of each line in (3)–(4).  \n\n*If \\(a,b\\ge2\\):* set \\(s=a+b\\).  \n\n- If \\(s\\) is odd, write \\(s=2i+1\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le m\\)).  \n  The integer points of \\(L_i\\) are \\((1+t,2i-t)\\) for \\(t=0,\\dots ,2i\\); each satisfies \\(a+b=2i+1=s\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_i\\).  \n\n- If \\(s\\) is even, write \\(s=2i+2\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le m\\)).  \n  The integer points of \\(M_i\\) are \\((1+t,2i+1-t)\\) for \\(t=0,\\dots ,2i+1\\); each satisfies \\(a+b=2i+2=s\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(M_i\\).  \n\n- The point \\((1,1)\\) (the only point with sum \\(2\\)) lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\) (5).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_k\\)