            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n-1\\;}\n\\]\n\n(the case \\(n=3\\) is included, because for \\(n=3\\) the condition “\\(k\\) odd with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)” gives only \\(k=1\\), which is impossible; consequently for \\(n=3\\) the only admissible value is \\(k=0\\)).  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular sub‑problem.**  \n   Use \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (these are non‑sunny).  \n   After deleting the points whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\) and translating the remaining points by \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\) we obtain the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the integer points of a right‑angled triangle of “size’’ \\(k\\).  \n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to:  \n\n   > **(P\\(_k\\))**  *Cover the set \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*\n\n2. **A forced non‑sunny line.**  \n   The points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) all lie on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   A sunny line is never horizontal, therefore each sunny line meets this row in at most one point.  \n   With only \\(n\\) lines we could cover the \\(n\\) points of the row only if **all** the lines were sunny, which would leave no non‑sunny line to cover the column \\(x=1\\).  \n   A similar argument with the column \\(x=1\\) shows that at least one line must be non‑sunny; consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Even \\(k>0\\) is impossible.**  \n   In any line the two integer points \\((a,b)\\) and \\((c,d)\\) satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{d-b}{c-a}\\neq -1\\qquad\\text{(otherwise the line would be parallel to }x+y=0).\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of each diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,k+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The diagonal \\(D_{k/2+2}\\) (which exists because \\(k\\) is even) contains an **odd** number of points, namely  \n\n   \\[\n   |D_{k/2+2}|=\\frac{k}{2}+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since a sunny line meets this diagonal in at most one point, covering all its points needs at least \\(\\frac{k}{2}+1\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n   Because we are allowed only \\(k\\) sunny lines, we would need  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge \\frac{k}{2}+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\ge 2 .\n   \\]\n\n   This inequality is true, but we obtain a stricter bound by looking at the *next* diagonal \\(D_{k/2+3}\\), which contains \\(\\frac{k}{2}+2\\) points.  \n   The same reasoning forces  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge \\frac{k}{2}+2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\ge 4 .\n   \\]\n\n   Continuing one step further gives the diagonal \\(D_{k/2+4}\\) with \\(\\frac{k}{2}+3\\) points and the requirement  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge \\frac{k}{2}+3\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\ge 6 .\n   \\]\n\n   Repeating this process we eventually obtain the impossible inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge \\frac{k}{2}+ \\frac{k}{2}+1 = k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The contradiction shows that **no even positive integer \\(k\\) can satisfy (P\\(_k\\)).**  \n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\text{ even }(>0)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\text{ impossible}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge 0)\\).  \n   Define the following \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *For each \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) set*  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; \\text{the line through }(1,2i+2)\\text{ and }(2i+3,1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1-(2i+2)}{(2i+3)-1}= -\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The integer points on \\(L_i\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (1+t,\\;2i+2-t),\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,2i+2,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. all points of the diagonal \\(D_{2i+3}\\) (the diagonal whose sum of coordinates equals the odd number \\(2i+3\\)).\n\n   *For each \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) set*  \n\n   \\[\n   M_i:\\; \\text{the line through }(1,2i+3)\\text{ and }(2i+4,1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1-(2i+3)}{(2i+4)-1}= -\\frac{2i+2}{2i+3}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1,\n   \\]\n\n   hence \\(M_i\\) is also sunny.  Its integer points are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1+t,\\;2i+3-t),\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,2i+3,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. all points of the diagonal \\(D_{2i+4}\\) (the diagonal whose sum equals the even number \\(2i+4\\)).\n\n   *Finally* add the line  \n\n   \\[\n   C:\\; y=x ,\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   whose slope is \\(1\\) (sunny) and which contains the unique point of the diagonal \\(D_{2}\\).\n\n   **Counting the lines.**  \n   We have \\(m\\) lines of type \\(L_i\\), \\(m\\) lines of type \\(M_i\\) and the line \\(C\\); altogether  \n\n   \\[\n   m+m+1 = 2m+1 = k\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines.\n\n   **Verification that the \\(k\\) lines cover \\(T_k\\).**  \n\n   *Diagonal \\(D_{2}\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\))* is on \\(C\\).  \n\n   *For each odd sum \\(s=2i+3\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le m-1\\)*, the line \\(L_i\\) contains **all** integer points of \\(D_s\\) (by the description of its integer points).  \n\n   *For each even sum \\(s=2i+4\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le m-1\\)*, the line \\(M_i\\) contains **all** integer points of \\(D_s\\).  \n\n   Since the diagonals \\(D_s\\) for \\(s=2,3,