            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n*Lemma 1 (a non‑sunny line is unavoidable).*\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(R\\) and \\(C\\) consist of \\(n\\) points that have to be covered.  \nA sunny line is never horizontal, hence it meets \\(R\\) in at most one point; similarly it meets \\(C\\) in at most one point.  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, each line could contribute at most one point to \\(R\\) and at most one point to \\(C\\).  \nSince \\(|R|=|C|=n\\), every line would have to meet both \\(R\\) and \\(C\\).  \nA line that meets \\(R\\) at \\((a,1)\\) and \\(C\\) at \\((1,b)\\) must contain the two distinct points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\); its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{b-1}{1-a}\\).  \nIf \\(a\\neq1\\) and \\(b\\neq1\\) this slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), but it equals \\(-1\\) exactly when \\(a+b=2\\).  \nBecause \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the only way to have slope \\(-1\\) is \\(a=b=1\\).  \nThus a line that meets both \\(R\\) and \\(C\\) at points different from \\((1,1)\\) would have slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden for a sunny line.  \nConsequently at most one line can meet both \\(R\\) and \\(C\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines can meet at most one of the two sets.  \nHence at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny.  This proves (1).\n\n*Lemma 2 (construction for odd \\(k\\)).*  \n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge0\\)).  Define the lines \\(L_i,M_i,C\\) as in the sketch.  \nWe verify each claim.\n\n1. **Sunnyness.**  \n   For any integer \\(i\\ge0\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{slope}(L_i)=-\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\n   \\qquad\n   \\operatorname{slope}(M_i)=-\\frac{2i+2}{2i+3}\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\n   \\]\n   and \\(\\operatorname{slope}(C)=1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence all are sunny.\n\n2. **Integer points on \\(L_i\\).**  \n   The line through \\((1,2i+2)\\) and \\((2i+3,1)\\) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   y-(2i+2)=\\frac{1-(2i+2)}{(2i+3)-1}\\,(x-1)\n          =-\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\,(x-1).\n   \\]\n   Solving for integer \\(x=1+t\\) (\\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\)) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y=2i+2-\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\,t.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\gcd(2i+1,2i+2)=1\\), the expression is integral exactly when \\(t\\) is a multiple of \\(2i+2\\).  Writing \\(t=s\\) with \\(0\\le s\\le 2i+2\\) yields the integer points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1+s,\\;2i+2-s),\\qquad s=0,1,\\dots ,2i+2,\n   \\]\n   which are precisely the points of the diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{2i+3}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2i+3,\\;a,b\\ge1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Integer points on \\(M_i\\).**  \n   An identical computation shows that the line through \\((1,2i+3)\\) and \\((2i+4,1)\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1+s,\\;2i+3-s),\\qquad s=0,1,\\dots ,2i+3,\n   \\]\n   i.e. the diagonal \\(D_{2i+4}\\).\n\n4. **Coverage of \\(T_k\\).**  \n   The set \\(T_k\\) is the disjoint union of the diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{k+1}.\n   \\]\n   By the previous items,\n   * \\(C\\) covers \\(D_{2}\\);\n   * for each \\(i=0,\\dots ,m-1\\), \\(L_i\\) covers \\(D_{2i+3}\\);\n   * for each \\(i=0,\\dots ,m-1\\), \\(M_i\\) covers \\(D_{2i+4}\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on one of the constructed lines.  The lines are distinct because each has a different slope, and there are exactly \\(k\\) of them.\n\n*Putting the pieces together.*\n\nTake any integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n* If \\(k=0\\) we simply use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all of them are non‑sunny and they cover every required point.\n\n* Let \\(k\\) be odd with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n  Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny).  \n  After removing the points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\) and translating the remaining points by \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\) we obtain the set \\(T_k\\).  \n  Lemma 2 provides \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover \\(T_k\\).  Adding back the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n* By Lemma 1 any admissible family must contain at least one non‑sunny line, so \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\nThus we have completely settled the cases \\(k=0\\) and all odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  The existence (or non‑existence) of configurations with an even number \\(k\\) of sunny lines for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) is not resolved by the arguments presented here.  Consequently the set of **known** admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n-1\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
