e0\\)).  Define the lines \\(L_i,M_i,C\\) as in the sketch.  \n> 1. **Sunnyness.**  \n>    For any integer \\(i\\ge0\\),  \n>    \\[\n>    \\operatorname{slope}(L_i)=-\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\n>    \\qquad\n>    \\operatorname{slope}(M_i)=-\\frac{2i+2}{2i+3}\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\n>    \\]  \n>    and \\(\\operatorname{slope}(C)=1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n> 2. **Integer points on \\(L_i\\).**  \n>    The line through \\((1,2i+2)\\) and \\((2i+3,1)\\) has equation  \n>    \\[\n>    y-(2i+2)=\\frac{1-(2i+2)}{(2i+3)-1}\\,(x-1)\n>           =-\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\,(x-1).\n>    \\]  \n>    Solving for integer \\(x=1+t\\) (\\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\)) gives  \n>    \\[\n>    y=2i+2-\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\,t.\n>    \\]  \n>    Because \\(\\gcd(2i+1,2i+2)=1\\), the expression is integral exactly when \\(t\\) is a multiple of \\(2i+2\\).  Writing \\(t=s\\) with \\(0\\le s\\le 2i+2\\) yields the integer points  \n>    \\[\n>    (1+s,\\;2i+2-s),\\qquad s=0,1,\\dots ,2i+2,\n>    \\]  \n>    which are precisely the points of the diagonal  \n>    \\[\n>    D_{2i+3}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2i+3,\\;a,b\\ge1\\}.\n>    \\]  \n> 3. **Integer points on \\(M_i\\).**  \n>    An identical computation shows that the line through \\((1,2i+3)\\) and \\((2i+4,1)\\) contains exactly the points  \n>    \\[\n>    (1+s,\\;2i+3-s),\\qquad s=0,1,\\dots ,2i+3,\n>    \\]  \n>    i.e. the diagonal \\(D_{2i+4}\\).  \n> 4. **Coverage of \\(T_k\\).**  \n>    The set \\(T_k\\) is the disjoint union of the diagonals  \n>    \\[\n>    D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{k+1}.\n>    \\]  \n>    By the previous items,\n>    * \\(C\\) covers \\(D_{2}\\);\n>    * for each \\(i=0,\\dots ,m-1\\), \\(L_i\\) covers \\(D_{2i+3}\\);\n>    * for each \\(i=0,\\dots ,m-1\\), \\(M_i\\) covers \\(D_{2i+4}\\).\u201d\n\n#### Verification\n\n1. **Sunnyness of the slopes.**  \n   - For any integer \\(i\\ge0\\), \\(-\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\) is a negative rational number not equal to \\(0\\), not infinite, and not \\(-1\\) (since \\(\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\neq1\\)). Similarly \\(-\\frac{2i+2}{2i+3}\\neq-1\\). The slope of \\(C\\) is \\(1\\). Hence each listed line is indeed sunny. This part is correct.\n\n2. **Claim about integer points on \\(L_i\\).**  \n   - The line \\(L_i\\) is defined as the line through \\((1,2i+2)\\) and \\((2i+3,1)\\). The derived equation is correct:\n     \\[\n     y = 2i+2 - \\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}(x-1).\n     \\]\n   - Substituting \\(x=1+t\\) yields \\(y = 2i+2 - \\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}t\\).  \n\n   - The argument that \\(y\\) is integral iff \\(t\\) is a multiple of \\(2i+2\\) is **incorrect**. Since \\(\\gcd(2i+1,2i+2)=1\\), the fraction \\(\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}t\\) is integral **iff** \\(2i+2\\) divides \\(t\\). This part is correct.  \n\n   - However, the range for \\(t\\) is not correctly described. The line is defined for all real \\(x\\); to obtain integer points with both coordinates \\(\\ge1\\) and satisfying the condition \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) (or the appropriate bound), we must consider all integer multiples \\(t = (2i+2)q\\) where \\(q\\) can be any integer such that the resulting point lies in the required region. The solution restricts \\(t\\) to \\(0\\le s\\le 2i+2\\) (i.e., \\(q=0\\) or \\(q=1\\)), thereby producing only the points\n     \\[\n     (1+s,\\;2i+2-s),\\qquad s=0,1,\\dots ,2i+2.\n     \\]\n     These points indeed satisfy \\(a+b = (1+s)+(2i+2-s) = 2i+3\\).  \n\n   - **Critical flaw:** The line \\(L_i\\) also contains the point \\((2i+3,1)\\), which corresponds to \\(s = 2i+2\\) **plus** an extra step: plugging \\(t = (2i+2)(1) = 2i+2\\) yields \\(x = 1 + 2i+2 = 2i+3\\) and \\(y = 2i+2 - \\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}(2i+2) = 2i+2 - (2i+1) = 1\\). This point is indeed included in the list (it corresponds to \\(s = 2i+2\\)). However, the line also contains points with \\(t = (2i+2)q\\) for **any integer** \\(q\\). For \\(q=2\\) we obtain \\((4i+5, -2i)\\), which has a negative \\(y\\)-coordinate and thus lies outside the required region, but the existence of such points does not affect the claim that the line contains **exactly** the diagonal \\(D_{2i+3}\\).  \n\n   - The crucial mistake is the assertion that the set of integer points on \\(L_i\\) **is precisely** the diagonal \\(D_{2i+3}\\). While the points listed indeed lie on the diagonal, the line also contains the point \\((2i+3,1)\\) whose sum of coordinates is \\(2i+4\\), **not** \\(2i+3\\). Hence the diagonal \\(D_{2i+3}\\) is **not** the full set of integer points on \\(L_i\\); the line misses the point \\((2,2i+1)\\) (which belongs to the diagonal) unless \\(i=0\\). For example, with \\(i=0\\), \\(L_0\\) passes through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\); the diagonal \\(D_3\\) consists of \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\). The point \\((2,1)\\) is **not** on \\(L_0\\). Therefore the claim that \\(L_i\\) covers the entire diagonal \\(D_{2i+3}\\) is false.\n\n   - Consequently, the construction does **not** guarantee coverage of all points in the diagonal \\(D_{2i+3}\\).\n\n3. **Claim about integer points on \\(M_i\\).**  \n   - The same erroneous reasoning is applied to the line \\(M_i\\) (through \\((1,2i+3)\\) and \\((2i+4,1)\\)). By analogous computation, the line contains the points \\((1+s,\\,2i+3-s)\\) for \\(s=0,\\dots,2i+3\\), which lie on the diagonal \\(D_{2i+4}\\). However, the line also contains the endpoint \\((2i+4,1)\\) whose coordinate sum is \\(2i+5\\), not \\(2i+4\\). Moreover, the diagonal \\(D_{2i+4}\\) includes points such as \\((2,2i+2)\\) which are not on \\(M_i\\). Hence the claim that \\(M_i\\) covers the entire diagonal \\(D_{2i+4}\\) is likewise false.\n\n4. **Coverage of \\(T_k\\).**  \n   - The set \\(T_k\\) is defined as the collection of all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\). This set indeed decomposes into the disjoint union of diagonals \\(D_2, D_3, \\dots, D_{k+1}\\).  \n   - The solution asserts that the constructed lines \\(C, L_i, M_i\\) cover each diagonal respectively. Because the previous claims about \\(L_i\\) and \\(M_i\\) are false, the coverage argument collapses. In particular, for \\(k=3\\) (i.e., \\(m=1\\)), the construction would use \\(C\\) to cover \\(D_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\), \\(L_0\\) to cover \\(D_3=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\), and \\(M_0\\) to cover \\(D_4=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\). As shown, \\(L_0\\) does **not** contain \\((2,1)\\) and \\(M_0\\) does **not** contain \\((2,2)\\) or \\((3,1)\\). Hence the claimed coverage fails even for the smallest odd \\(k\\).\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the construction does not actually cover the required points; the statements about the integer points on \\(L_i\\) and \\(M_i\\) are false.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \u201cPutting the pieces together\u201d \u2013 use of Lemma\u202f2 and vertical lines\n\n**Quoted passage**\n\n> \u201cTake any integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n> * If \\(k=0\\) we simply use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all of them are non\u2011sunny and they cover every required point.  \n> * Let \\(k\\) be odd with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n>   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n>   After removing the points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\) and translating the remaining points by \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\) we obtain the set \\(T_k\\).  \n>   Lemma\u202f2 provides \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover \\(T_k\\).  Adding back the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\u201d\n\n#### Verification\n\n1. **Case \\(k=0\\).**  \n   - Using the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) indeed covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n\\). Since the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n\\), every required point lies on one of these vertical lines. The lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, thus non\u2011sunny. This part is correct.\n\n2. **Odd \\(k\\) construction.**  \n   - The idea is to first cover the points with small \\(x\\)-coordinate using vertical lines, then handle the remaining points via the construction of Lemma\u202f2.  \n   - The translation step is described but not justified: after deleting points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\), the remaining points have first coordinate in \\(\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). Subtracting \\(n-k\\) from the first coordinate maps them to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\). However, the condition on the sum changes: originally we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); after translation we obtain points \\((a',b)\\) with \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and the inequality becomes \\((a'+n-k)+b\\le n+1\\) \u21d4 \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\). Hence the translated set is exactly \\(T_k\\). This reasoning is correct, though it is not explicitly shown in the solution (a **Justification Gap**).  \n\n   - **Critical problem:** Lemma\u202f2 does **not** correctly cover \\(T_k\\) (as shown above). Therefore the claim that \u201cLemma\u202f2 provides \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover \\(T_k\\)\u201d is false. Consequently the entire odd\u2011\\(k\\) construction fails.\n\n3. **Distinctness of lines.**  \n   - Assuming Lemma\u202f2 were correct, the vertical lines have slope \\(\\infty\\) (non\u2011sunny) and the sunny lines have distinct finite slopes, so the lines would be distinct. This part would be fine, but it is moot because the covering claim is false.\n\n**Classification:** The translation step has a **Justification Gap**, but the overall construction is invalid due to the critical error in Lemma\u202f2.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Final conclusion about admissible \\(k\\)\n\n**Quoted passage**\n\n> \u201cThus we have completely settled the cases \\(k=0\\) and all odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  The existence (or non\u2011existence) of configurations with an even number \\(k\\) of sunny lines for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) is not resolved by the arguments presented here.  Consequently the set of **known** admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n-1\\;}.\n> \\]\u201d\n\n#### Verification\n\n- The problem explicitly asks