sserts: “the slope equals \\(-1\\) exactly when \\(a+b=2\\).”  \n   - Correct condition: \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{b-1}{1-a}=-1\\) ⇔ \\(b-1 = -(1-a)\\) ⇔ \\(b-1 = a-1\\) ⇔ \\(b=a\\).  \n   - Thus the slope is \\(-1\\) **iff** \\(a=b\\), not when \\(a+b=2\\). The statement in the solution is false.  \n\n   **Impact:** Because the condition for a forbidden slope is mis‑identified, the subsequent claim that “the only way to have slope \\(-1\\) is \\(a=b=1\\)” is also false. In fact, any pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a=b\\ge2\\) would give slope \\(-1\\), but those pairs are allowed for a sunny line only if the slope is not \\(-1\\); the argument that such pairs cannot occur is therefore invalid.\n\n6. **Conclusion “at most one line can meet both \\(R\\) and \\(C\\)”.**  \n   - The reasoning that only the line through \\((1,1)\\) can meet both sets without having slope \\(-1\\) is based on the incorrect condition \\(a+b=2\\). In reality, many lines (e.g., through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) with slope \\(-2\\)) meet both \\(R\\) and \\(C\\) and are sunny. Hence the claim “at most one line can meet both \\(R\\) and \\(C\\)” is false.\n\n7. **Result “at least one line must be non‑sunny”.**  \n   - Since the premise leading to this conclusion is false, the conclusion does not follow. Counterexample: for \\(n=3\\) the three lines  \n     \\[\n     \\ell_1:\\; y=x,\\quad \\ell_2:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\quad \\ell_3:\\; y=-2x+5\n     \\]  \n     are all sunny and together cover all required points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le4\\). Thus the bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) is disproved.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** – the slope mis‑calculation invalidates Lemma 1 and the derived bound \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Lemma 2 – “construction for odd \\(k\\)”\n\n**Quoted passage (selected parts)**\n\n> “Let \\(k=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge0\\)).  Define the lines \\(L_i,M_i,C\\) as in the sketch.  \n> 1. **Sunnyness.**  \n>    For any integer \\(i\\ge0\\),  \n>    \\[\n>    \\operatorname{slope}(L_i)=-\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\n>    \\qquad\n>    \\operatorname{slope}(M_i)=-\\frac{2i+2}{2i+3}\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\n>    \\]  \n>    and \\(\\operatorname{slope}(C)=1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n> 2. **Integer points on \\(L_i\\).**  \n>    The line through \\((1,2i+2)\\) and \\((2i+3,1)\\) has equation  \n>    \\[\n>    y-(2i+2)=\\frac{1-(2i+2)}{(2i+3)-1}\\,(x-1)\n>           =-\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\,(x-1).\n>    \\]  \n>    Solving for integer \\(x=1+t\\) (\\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\)) gives  \n>    \\[\n>    y=2i+2-\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\,t.\n>    \\]  \n>    Because \\(\\gcd(2i+1,2i+2)=1\\), the expression is integral exactly when \\(t\\) is a multiple of \\(2i+2\\).  Writing \\(t=s\\) with \\(0\\le s\\le 2i+2\\) yields the integer points  \n>    \\[\n>    (1+s,\\;2i+2-s),\\qquad s=0,1,\\dots ,2i+2,\n>    \\]  \n>    which are precisely the points of the diagonal  \n>    \\[\n>    D_{2i+3}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=2i+3,\\;a,b\\ge1\\}.\n>    \\]  \n> 3. **Integer points on \\(M_i\\).**  \n>    An identical computation shows that the line through \\((1,2i+3)\\) and \\((2i+4,1)\\) contains exactly the points  \n>    \\[\n>    (1+s,\\;2i+3-s),\\qquad s=0,1,\\dots ,2i+3,\n>    \\]  \n>    i.e. the diagonal \\(D_{2i+4}\\).  \n> 4. **Coverage of \\(T_k\\).**  \n>    The set \\(T_k\\) is the disjoint union of the diagonals  \n>    \\[\n>    D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{k+1}.\n>    \\]  \n>    By the previous items,\n>    * \\(C\\) covers \\(D_{2}\\);\n>    * for each \\(i=0,\\dots ,m-1\\), \\(L_i\\) covers \\(D_{2i+3}\\);\n>    * for each \\(i=0,\\dots ,m-1\\), \\(M_i\\) covers \\(D_{2i+4}\\).”\n\n#### Verification\n\n1. **Sunnyness of the slopes.**  \n   - For any integer \\(i\\ge0\\), \\(-\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\) is a negative rational number not equal to \\(0\\), not infinite, and not \\(-1\\) (since \\(\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}\\neq1\\)). Similarly \\(-\\frac{2i+2}{2i+3}\\neq-1\\). The slope of \\(C\\) is \\(1\\). Hence each listed line is indeed sunny. This part is correct.\n\n2. **Claim about integer points on \\(L_i\\).**  \n   - The line \\(L_i\\) is defined as the line through \\((1,2i+2)\\) and \\((2i+3,1)\\). The derived equation is correct:\n     \\[\n     y = 2i+2 - \\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}(x-1).\n     \\]\n   - Substituting \\(x=1+t\\) yields \\(y = 2i+2 - \\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}t\\).  \n\n   - The argument that \\(y\\) is integral iff \\(t\\) is a multiple of \\(2i+2\\) is **incorrect**. Since \\(\\gcd(2i+1,2i+2)=1\\), the fraction \\(\\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}t\\) is integral **iff** \\(2i+2\\) divides \\(t\\). This part is correct.  \n\n   - However, the range for \\(t\\) is not correctly described. The line is defined for all real \\(x\\); to obtain integer points with both coordinates \\(\\ge1\\) and satisfying the condition \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) (or the appropriate bound), we must consider all integer multiples \\(t = (2i+2)q\\) where \\(q\\) can be any integer such that the resulting point lies in the required region. The solution restricts \\(t\\) to \\(0\\le s\\le 2i+2\\) (i.e., \\(q=0\\) or \\(q=1\\)), thereby producing only the points\n     \\[\n     (1+s,\\;2i+2-s),\\qquad s=0,1,\\dots ,2i+2.\n     \\]\n     These points indeed satisfy \\(a+b = (1+s)+(2i+2-s) = 2i+3\\).  \n\n   - **Critical flaw:** The line \\(L_i\\) also contains the point \\((2i+3,1)\\), which corresponds to \\(s = 2i+2\\) **plus** an extra step: plugging \\(t = (2i+2)(1) = 2i+2\\) yields \\(x = 1 + 2i+2 = 2i+3\\) and \\(y = 2i+2 - \\frac{2i+1}{2i+2}(2i+2) = 2i+2 - (2i+1) = 1\\). This point is indeed included in the list (it corresponds to \\(s = 2i+2\\)). However, the line also contains points with \\(t = (2i+2)q\\) for **any integer** \\(q\\). For \\(q=2\\) we obtain \\((4i+5, -2i)\\), which has a negative \\(y\\)-coordinate and thus lies outside the required region, but the existence of such points does not affect the claim that the line contains **exactly** the diagonal \\(D_{2i+3}\\).  \n\n   - The crucial mistake is the assertion that the set of integer points on \\(L_i\